content.text:
The crosshair is there: top vertical line, left horizontal line, and dot — but the right and bottom arms are missing? With `.mining` class, crosshair spans gold — no. Looking at crop: vertical line at top center, horizontal line at left center, and white dot. So it's an L-shape rather than a plus. The CSS defines only two spans: span 1 = vertical top (8px), span 2 = horizontal left (8px). There's no right/bottom! I designed it as a minimal L-shaped crosshair with a dot. Hmm, that's intentional? Based on CSS:

#crosshair span:nth-child(1) { left: 50%; top: 2px; ... } (top tick)
#crosshair span:nth-child(2) { top: 50%; left: 2px; ... } (left tick)
i = center dot.

So it's an L-shaped crosshair (top + left + dot). That's an asymmetric design mistake — likely intended to be a plus. For a mining game, a proper plus crosshair is more readable. Let me add right and bottom ticks by extending the CSS using pseudo-elements? We have only two spans. Could style #crosshair::before and ::after for right/bottom. Or change two spans to also have box-shadow? Simplest: use ::before/::after on #crosshair for the right and bottom ticks. Hmm, but children already; pseudo-elements of #crosshair could be absolute. Let's define:

#crosshair::before, #crosshair::after {
 content: ""; position: absolute; background: ...; border-radius: 1px;
}
#crosshair::before { left: 50%; bottom: 2px; width: 2px; height: 8px; transform: translateX(-50%); }
#crosshair::after { right: 2px; top: 50%; width: 8px; height: 2px; transform: translateY(-50%); }

And add the same box-shadow. Also mining state should turn them gold. Currently #crosshair.mining span, i only. Add pseudo.

Let me update CSS. Also maybe the crosshair dot 3px. Good.

Now fix the arm placement and held block. Then final check. Let me edit.

